.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Arrhenius&#x27;s k = Ae^{-E_a/RT} explains why a catalyst&#x27;s lower activation energy multiplies the rate by e^{\Delta E_a/RT} (~chemistrykit.surface.systems.catalysis.compare_catalyzed_rate). Ostwald&#x27;s definition adds the key condition that a catalyst cannot move the equilibrium. It lowers the barrier for the forward and the reverse reaction by the same amount, so both rate constants grow by the same factor and K = k_f/k_r does not change. Below, the reversible reaction A \rightleftharpoons B reaches equilibrium far sooner with the catalyst, and it reaches exactly the same equilibrium.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/surface/catalysis/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_arrhenius_ostwald_rate_enhancement_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/surface/catalysis/plot_02_arrhenius_ostwald_rate_enhancement`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Arrhenius and Ostwald: a catalyst speeds a reaction but leaves its equilibrium alone</div>
